catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ 02 — effects, bottom-up.
\
\ THE CLAIM (D01 §1, D-01): a word performs an operation and says so in its
\ type. What the operation MEANS is not decided there; it is supplied by
\ whoever handles it further out. So one program text has as many meanings as
\ you care to write handlers for, and the program never mentions any of them.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ --- An effect is a set of operation signatures. `declare` requires one:
\ there is no body to infer it from.
catcat> effect Log { declare say ( str -- ) }
effect Log say
catcat> \ --- Words that log. The effect appears in the type and PROPAGATES: only
\ `step1` and `step2` mention `say`, yet `pipeline` picks up `!Log` without
\ being asked, because a written stack effect leaves the row to be inferred.
catcat> define step1    ( i64 -- i64 !Log ) { "  squaring\n" say dup * }
define step2    ( i64 -- i64 !Log ) { "  adding one\n" say 1 + }
define pipeline ( i64 -- i64 )      { step1 step2 }
defined step1 ( i64 -- i64 !Log )
defined step2 ( i64 -- i64 !Log )
defined pipeline ( i64 -- i64 !Log )
catcat> \ The propagation is not advisory. A word that claims to be pure and calls one
\ that is not is rejected — this is the "must be specified up the line" half of
\ the claim, enforced.
catcat> define sneaky ( i64 -- i64 ! ) { pipeline }
error: sneaky declares no effects but its body has !Log
catcat> \ Unhandled, the operation escapes to the host, which has no implementation
\ for a user-declared effect and says so.
catcat> 3 pipeline
unhandled: say escaped with no handler in scope
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Now the same four words, four times, unmodified.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ --- (a) Print it. The handler turns `say` into IO, so the handled
\ expression is `!IO` — the row records what the MEANING costs, not what the
\ program asked for.
catcat> "(a) printing:\n" print
handle Log over ( ) init { } { say { print } } { 3 pipeline } show print "\n" print
(a) printing:
  squaring
  adding one
10
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- (b) Throw it away. Now the same expression is pure. Not "pure except
\ for logging" — pure, and a caller may treat it as such.
catcat> "(b) discarding:\n" print
handle Log over ( ) init { } { say { pop } } { 3 pipeline } show print "\n" print
(b) discarding:
10
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- (c) Count the messages. A handler may carry STATE, which is what makes
\ it an object rather than a function (D-36): `over ( i64 )` declares the
\ state segment, `init` gives its value, and each implementation is checked at
\ `( state args -- state results )` with the state on TOP.
\
\ On exit the handler leaves its final state above the result.
catcat> "(c) counting: " print
handle Log over ( i64 ) init { 0 } { say { swap pop 1 + } } { 3 pipeline }
show print " messages, result " print show print "\n" print
(c) counting: 2 messages, result 10
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- (d) Capture it. Same shape as (c), with a `str` for state instead of a
\ counter — so the log becomes a VALUE, with no file, no buffer and no IO.
catcat> "(d) capturing:\n" print
handle Log over ( str ) init { "" } { say { swap cat } } { 3 pipeline }
swap pop print
(d) capturing:
  squaring
  adding one
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ Two more properties worth seeing.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ --- Handlers LAYER. An implementation may itself perform an operation, so a
\ handler is a transformer and not only a sink: this one adds a tag and passes
\ the message on to a second effect, which the enclosing handler prints. The
\ program in the middle is still `pipeline`, still unmodified.
catcat> effect Audit { declare note ( str -- ) }
effect Audit note
catcat> "(e) layered — inner tags, outer prints:\n" print
handle Audit over ( ) init { } { note { print } } {
  handle Log over ( ) init { } { say { "    [tagged] " swap cat note } } { 3 pipeline }
} show print "\n" print
(e) layered — inner tags, outer prints:
    [tagged]   squaring
    [tagged]   adding one
10
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- What is NOT allowed is a handler re-entering itself. Its state is lent
\ to the implementation while that runs, so an operation that reaches the same
\ frame finds no state and the machine stops. This is a DYNAMIC check in a
\ language whose linearity is otherwise static, and it is tracked as Q-12 in
\ NOTES/N02_Open_Questions.md. Failing loudly is the acceptable direction.
catcat> handle Log over ( ) init { } { say { "x" swap cat say } } { 3 pipeline }
STUCK: handler re-entered while its own state is in use
catcat> \ --- A hole, shown deliberately. A handler that implements NONE of its
\ effect's operations still discharges the effect from the row, while the
\ machine forwards the operation outward. So this typechecks as pure...
catcat> define notpure ( i64 -- i64 ! ) { handle Log over ( ) init { } { } { pipeline } }
defined notpure ( i64 -- i64 )
catcat> \ ...and still escapes at run time. That is how `unsafe` and `handle Rec` work
\ — those effects have no operations to leave unimplemented — but for `Log` it
\ is a real gap, tracked as Q-16. A demo should show its language's holes.
catcat> 3 notpure
unhandled: say escaped with no handler in scope
